<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">De Administrando Imperio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 950</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De Administrando Imperio is a Greek handbook on foreign policy compiled around 950 by the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> Porphyrogennetos for his son Romanos. It describes the peoples around the empire, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, Rus', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Hungarians, and advises on how to deal with them. It is a key source for the early history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and the steppe peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio.jpg" alt="De Administrando Imperio" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wooofer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_(2).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
